3. Tight; firm; sound; in good condition or health.

{{smaller block|Some o' them will be sent back to fling the earth into the hole, and make a' thing ''trig'' again. ''Scott'', Antiquary, xxiv.}}

{{smaller block|I never heard a more devilish pother. I wish I was in mid-ocean all ''trig'' and tight. Then I would enjoy such a passion of wind. {{right|''A. E. Barr'', Friend Olivia, xvii.}}}}

4. Neat; tidy; trim; spruce; smart.

{{smaller block|Auld Reekie aye he keepit tight,
An' ''trig'' an' braw;
But now they'll busk her like a fright—
Willie's awa'! ''Burns'', To W. Creech.}}

{{smaller block|The stylish gait and air of the ''trig'' little body.
{{right|''The Century'', XXVIII. 541.}}}}

5. Active; clever. ''Halliwell.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' A dandy; a coxcomb.

{{smaller block|You are . . . a ''trig'',
And an Amadis de Gaul, or a Don Quixote.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, iv. 4.}}}}

[Obsolete, provincial, or colloq. in all uses.]

'''trig'''<sup>1</sup> (trig), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''trigged'', ppr. ''trigging.'' [< ''trig''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] To dress; trick: with ''up.'' ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''trig'''<sup>2</sup> (trig), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''trigged'', ppr. ''trigging.'' [< Dan. ''trykke'' = Sw. ''trycka'' = OHG. ''drucchen'', MHG. ''drücken'', ''drucken'', G. ''drücken'', ''drucken'' = AS. ''thryccan'', press.] To fill; stuff; cram. ''Grose''; ''Brockett.'' [Obsolete or prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|By how much the more a man's skin is full ''trig'd'' with}}

{{smaller block|lawfull to marry; but bigamy is forbidden them, and ''trigamy'' detested in the Laiety. ''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 64.}}

{{smaller block|It is what he calls ''trigamy'', Madam, or the marrying of three wives, so that good old men may be solaced at once by the companionship of the wisdom of maturity, and of those less perfected but hardly less engaging qualities which are found at an earlier period of life.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, i.}}}}

'''trigastric''' (trī-gas′trik), ''a.'' [< Gr. τρεῖς (τρι-), three, + γαστήρ (γαστρ-), belly.] In ''anat.'', having three fleshy bellies, as a muscle.

'''trigeminal''' (trī-jem′i-nal), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''trigeminus'', three at a birth (see ''trigeminous''), + ''-al.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' 1. In ''anat.'' and ''zoöl.'', triple, triune, or threefold: specifically noting the trifacial or fifth cranial nerve (which see, under ''trifacial''). Also ''trigeminous.''—2. Of or pertaining to the trigeminal nerve: as, a ''trigeminal'' foramen.

{{smaller block|A preliminary stage of ''trigeminal'' neuralgia.
{{right|''Buck's Handbook of Med. Sciences'', III. 16.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' The trigeminal nerve; the trigeminus. See ''trifacial.''

'''trigemini''', ''n.'' Plural of ''trigeminus.''

'''trigeminous''' (trī-jem′i-nus), ''a.'' [< L. ''trigeminus'', three at a birth, triple, < ''tres'' (''tri-''), three, + ''geminus'', a twin: see ''geminous.''] 1. Being one of three born together; born three at a time. —2. In ''anat.'' and ''zoöl.'', same as ''trigeminal.''

'''trigeminus''' (trī-jem′i-nus), ''n.''; pl. ''trigemini'' (-nī). [NL., < L. ''trigeminus'', three at a birth: see ''trigeminous.''] In ''zoöl.'' and ''anat.'', the trifacial nerve. See ''trifacial.''

'''trigent†''', ''n.'' Same as ''trigon''<sup>2</sup>. ''Kersey'', 1708; ''Bailey'', 1731.

